{
  "completion": "Okay, let's tackle this problem. So, I need to forecast the probability that at least one of the remaining Millennium Prize Problems (excluding Poincar\u00e9 since it's already solved) will be solved by a certain date, with AI contributing to the solution. The forecast date is 2026-02-24. The deadlines are 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, 2050.\n\nFirst, I should recall the six problems left: Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, P vs NP, Riemann, Yang-Mills. All are extremely difficult. Historically, Millennium problems are intractable, but P vs NP has a high profile. Maybe AI could help here, especially with pattern recognition in proofs. But solving any of these requires deep new insights.\n\nAI's role: Current AI like LLMs can assist in conjecturing or verifying steps, but full proofs are still human-driven. Future AI systems might be more capable. By 2030, maybe AI can generate non-trivial lemmas or connections. However, verifying the entire proof would still need rigorous checking. Human-AI collaboration is key here.\n\nLooking at each deadline:\n\n- 2026: Only a few months left. Unlikely any major breakthrough so soon. Maybe 5%? But AI contributions need to be identifiable. Maybe very low.\n- 2030: 4 years from 2026. AI might have improved, but these problems are deep. Maybe 20%?\n- 2035: 9 years. AI tools could be more integrated. Maybe 45%?\n- 2040: 14 years. Perhaps more likely, but still challenging. 65%?\n- 2050: 24 years. If AI advances significantly, maybe 80%. But there's uncertainty.\n\nWait, need to make sure probabilities are non-decreasing. Also, account for the possibility it never happens. So 2050 can't be 100%.\n\nBut historical context: Only one Millennium problem (Poincar\u00e9) was solved in the first 10+ years. The others take decades. However, with AI, maybe acceleration. But some problems might be beyond AI. P vs NP is a candidate because it's discrete and logic-based, more amenable to formal verification. Navier-Stokes involves analysis which is harder.\n\nMaybe P vs NP has a higher chance. But whether AI can contribute an identifiable step. For example, AI could suggest a reduction or a new algorithm, but proving it's correct is the challenge.\n\nAlternatively, Yang-Mills mass gap is in physics, maybe AI can model it better, but the proof requires rigorous math.\n\nI think the highest chance is P vs NP. Let's adjust probabilities based on that. But since the question is about any of the six, even if others are less likely, the combination might increase the probability.\n\nBut each problem is extremely hard. Maybe the probability increases over time but plateaus. Let's think:\n\n2026: Very low, since only a few months left. 0.05 (5%)\n2030: Maybe 0.2 (20%)\n2035: 0.4 (40%)\n2040: 0.6 (60%)\n2050: 0.8 (80%)\n\nBut considering that AI might take longer to be trusted in math proofs. Verification could be a bottleneck. Maybe the probabilities are a bit lower.\n\nAlternatively, by 2040, AI systems capable of handling long proofs might exist. Maybe 0.5 by 2035, 0.7 by 2040, 0.85 by 2050.\n\nWait, another angle: The breakthroughs might have to come from AI systems far beyond current capabilities, which might take until 2040 or later. But some people are optimistic about AGI in the 2030s. If AGI exists, maybe 2035-2040.\n\nBut combining all factors, maybe:\n\n2026: 0.02\n2030: 0.15\n2035: 0.40\n2040: 0.60\n2050: 0.80\n\nBut I need to ensure non-decreasing. Also, maybe 2050 can't be too high if the problems are truly unsolvable. But the question includes \"account for possibility the event never occurs\", so probabilities less than 1.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps more conservative:\n\n2026: 0.01\n2030: 0.1\n2035: 0.3\n2040: 0.5\n2050: 0.7\n\nBut how to balance optimism about AI with the difficulty of the problems. I think the median estimates from experts on AI's impact on math might suggest a gradual increase. Maybe the final probabilities should reflect that while AI will help, the problems are so hard that even with AI, it's not guaranteed.\n\nAnother point: The requirement is that AI contributes an identifiable new mathematical argument or proof step. So even if humans solve it, but AI provided a key lemma, that counts. So as AI tools become better at generating proofs, the chances increase.\n\nLooking at current trends, AI is already used in math (e.g., suggesting conjectures, checking proofs). In 5-10 years, maybe more sophisticated. So by 2035, maybe 40%. By 2040, 60%. By 2050, 75% or so.\n\nBut I need to make sure the numbers are non-decreasing and between 0 and 1. Let me check:\n\n2026: 0.05 (since it's only a few months from forecast date 2026-02-24, deadline is end of 2026, so very low chance)\n2030: 0.20\n2035: 0.45\n2040: 0.65\n2050: 0.85\n\nBut maybe 2050 should be lower. Some problems might remain unsolved indefinitely. However, the question says \"at least one\". So even if 5 are unsolved, if one is solved, it counts. So the probability should be higher.\n\nBut considering all factors, I'll go with this estimate.\n</think>\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.05,\n  \"2030\": 0.2,\n  \"2035\": 0.45,\n  \"2040\": 0.65,\n  \"2050\": 0.85\n}",
